<figure class="thread-media thread-media--linked"><a href="/assets/archive/internet/goobec/goobec01-tratado-300kb.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open the Goobec class photograph at full size"><img src="/assets/media/thread/goobec-class-2009.webp" width="1200" height="900" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Goobec Google AdWords class in 2009, with Marcelo Nicchio among the participants."></a><figcaption><strong>Goobec Google AdWords class, 2009.</strong><span class="thread-media-note">Original period photograph, digitally restored with AI assistance.</span><a class="thread-media-open" href="/assets/archive/internet/goobec/goobec01-tratado-300kb.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open full-size image ↗</a></figcaption></figure><p>In 2008, Nicchio began managing small Google AdWords accounts intuitively and through self-directed learning, including a traditional restaurant on Marginal Tietê, a yoga studio, a buffet service, a painting atelier and an insurance brokerage. These were small operations, but they allowed him to work directly with campaign structure, keywords, segmentation, costs and return.</p><p>That same year, he met Rio de Janeiro-based professional <strong>Louise Martins</strong> during one of her visits to São Paulo. At a café, Louise opened her laptop and showed him an AdWords operation on a scale Nicchio had not yet encountered: a large account structured simultaneously across different countries. The scale of that operation made a strong impression. Search no longer looked only like a solution learned to address Mirantte's traffic problem; it began to appear as a much larger professional field.</p><p>Soon afterward, Louise would be featured in <strong>“Com a grife do Google”</strong>, an article published by <em>INFO</em> magazine in July 2008 about the then-small and competitive community of Google Advertising Professional — GAP certified professionals. <a href="https://datassette.org/revistas/info-informatica-revistas/info-exame-no-269" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">INFO Exame no. 269 · Jul. 2008 ↗</a></p><figure class="thread-media thread-media--linked"><a href="/assets/archive/internet/search_louisemartins_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Open the INFO page featuring Louise Martins at full size"><img src="/assets/archive/internet/search_louisemartins_01.jpg" width="608" height="800" loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="Page from INFO magazine no. 269, July 2008, featuring Louise Martins in the article “Com a grife do Google”."></a><figcaption><strong>INFO no. 269 · Jul. 2008 — “Com a grife do Google”.</strong><span class="thread-media-note">Page from the article featuring Louise Martins, a contemporary record of the Google Advertising Professional — GAP community.</span><a class="thread-media-open" href="/assets/archive/internet/search_louisemartins_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open full-size image ↗</a></figcaption></figure><p>The experience reinforced Nicchio's decision to enter the field professionally. In <strong>2009</strong>, he enrolled in one of the first Google AdWords classes at <strong>Goobec</strong>, founded in 2008 as Google's first official training partner in Latin America. During the immersion course taught by <strong>João Dalla</strong>, he met another student: <strong>Herik Mourão</strong>. <a href="https://www.goobec.com.br/sobre-a-goobec" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Goobec history ↗</a></p><p>After the course, he continued managing small accounts while studying Google's technical documentation for advertisers and professionals intensively throughout 2009.</p>
